FILE: assets/css/modalDisplayer.css
FEATURE: modal-stacking
DATE: 2026-08-03
PRIOR STATE: as left by feature 'modal-displayer-verification'

================================================================================
CHANGE 1 — Style the content host by class as well as by id
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
.vModal .modalSpace #newModal{
  width:100%;
}

NEW_SECTION
/* #newModal is the pre-stacking id, kept on the first layer for existing selectors */
.vModal .modalSpace .vModalHost,
.vModal .modalSpace #newModal{
  width:100%;
}

REASON
The element holding the displayed modal used to be identified by id="newModal".
With stacking there is one such element per layer, and repeating an id across layers
is exactly the collision that made nesting destroy the underlying modal, so the host
is now identified by the class vModalHost.

The id selector is kept rather than replaced because the first layer still carries
id="newModal" for backward compatibility with consumer stylesheets and scripts
written against the pre-stacking DOM. Both selectors therefore need the same rule:
the class covers every layer, the id covers nothing extra but keeps the original
selector meaningful for anyone who copied it.

No other rule needed changing. .vModal already positions each overlay fixed at full
viewport size, which is what a per-layer overlay requires, and layer ordering is set
with an inline z-index in the module rather than in CSS, since it depends on the
layer's depth in the stack.
